anv: Xe2 decodes AV1 and HEVC up to 16384x16384 (media-driver Xe2 caps), AV1 frame-size buffers sized from the stream

media-driver decodes AV1 and HEVC up to 16Kx16K on Xe2 (media_softlet/linux/xe2_hpm_r0/decode/{av1,hevc}/ddi/
capstable_data_*_decode_xe2_hpm_r0_specific.h: VAConfigAttribMaxPictureWidth/Height = CODEC_16K_MAX_PIC_*; H.264 stays
at CODEC_4K_MAX_PIC_WIDTH). anv reported 4096x4096 for every decode profile and sized the AV1 frame-size buffers
(AVP tile line / tile column row stores, the per-image temporal MV buffer) for a fixed 4096x4096 frame. They now follow
the session's max coded extent and the image's extent (media-driver mhw_vdbox_avp_impl.h CalculateBufferSize:
sbPerFrmWid / sbPerFrmHgt / totalSbPerFrame), never smaller than before. The line row stores depend on the tile width,
which AV1 caps at 4096, and stay in the internal row-store cache as media-driver keeps them (GetRowstoreCachingAddrs).
A single VDBOX decodes 8K: media-driver uses virtual-tile scalability from 7680x4320 only when VE is enabled and
there are two VDBOXes (decode_scalability_option.cpp), single pipe otherwise.
Verified on the B580: 7680x4320 AV1 8-bit bit-exact (60/60 frames), 135 fps.

diff --git a/src/intel/vulkan/anv_video.c b/src/intel/vulkan/anv_video.c
